    lectureTitle: "1.1 Philosophy as analysis of categories",
    body: `# Philosophy as the analysis of the categories

We do not confront the world as an undifferentiated blur. We understand it in terms of certain **categories** — *person, statement, fact, event, cause, time, mind, perception, belief, knowledge, language, truth, value, law, justice, existence, impossibility*, and others like them. **Philosophy studies these categories. It delineates their structures. This is its sole function.** So we may define it sharply: *philosophy is the discipline that delineates the structures of the categories in terms of which we think about the world.*

## Surfacing the categories from our basic beliefs

We can locate many of these categories simply by stating a few of our most ordinary beliefs and noticing which notions we are forced to use (the categories are the emphasized expressions):

The world isn't homogeneous. It is articulated into *events* bearing various *causal* and, more generally, *spatiotemporal* relations to one another. Many of these events involve more or less persistent *things* (rocks, trees). Some of these things have *minds*. Most animate, mind-having beings have *sense-perceptions* — they see things, hear things. Most percipient creatures have *beliefs*. Some of these beliefs are *true*; others *false*. Some of the true ones are cases of *knowledge*. Some creatures communicate through *language*; mastery of a language makes it easier to communicate one's beliefs and enhances one's ability to reason. Rational creatures make *value-judgments*, judging actions good or bad, and regulate their conduct by systems of *law*, whose supposed purpose is to secure *justice*.

## The non-trivial job

Stating a categorial claim — "Smith is a person," "this is an event," "that caused this" — is trivial; we do it constantly and effortlessly. The non-trivial job philosophy takes on is to make explicit **what falling under the category amounts to** — to identify, as clearly and explicitly as possible, the conditions a thing must satisfy if it is to fall under a given category. What conditions must a creature's behavior meet to count as *knowing a language*? What must a sequence of events satisfy for one to *cause* another? Answering such questions is the whole of philosophy's work.

## What this makes philosophy

So far as philosophy succeeds in this endeavor, it makes clear what we are doing whenever we do anything cognitive at all — whether making a casual observation or carrying out an extended piece of abstract reasoning. Every cognitive act deploys some category; analyzing the category lays bare the precondition of the act. For this reason philosophy is **the analysis of the preconditions of all knowledge** — and, in the author's memorable phrase, **the analysis of analysis, the logic of logic, the science of science.** It does not add a new fact alongside the facts the sciences discover; it clarifies the framework within which any fact can be stated at all.`,
