    lectureTitle: "1.3 Philosophy and the other disciplines",
    body: `# The relationship of philosophy to other disciplines

The philosopher is interested in the **laws governing the laws.** He does not want to know what *in fact* holds. He wants to know what it would even *make sense* to claim could hold. In a phrase: **he wants to know the laws that the laws can't break.**

## Specifics versus categories

Because philosophical knowledge is meta-knowledge — knowledge about knowledge — the philosopher's relation to ordinary facts is oblique. The non-philosopher wants specifics: *What* happened? *When* did it happen? *What* did it cause? *How* did it cause it? The philosopher is interested in these questions only to the extent that knowing the answers helps him understand the *categories* — cause, place, time, and the rest — that underlie any such knowledge in the first place. For him the facts are evidence about the framework, not the prize.

## The ladder of interests

The point is best seen as a ladder. The stranded motorist wants his car to work; he does not care in the least *what* will get it going. The engineer is interested in exactly that — but the engineer is not entirely innocent of the motorist's epistemic parochialism: he has no interest in what the laws of physics are except so far as he must know them to create the right mechanisms. The physicist is the engineer's reverse: he wants knowledge of the mechanisms only to the extent that it gives him knowledge of the *laws* embodied in them.

Now extend the series one rung higher:

> **The philosopher is to the physicist what the physicist is to the engineer, and what the engineer is to the stranded motorist.**

The philosopher wants to know what causes what, and what mechanisms were involved, only to the extent that knowing this helps him understand *what it is for one thing to cause another* — only, that is, so far as it helps him know what one *knows* in knowing that one thing made another happen. Each rung is interested in the rung below only as a means to the more abstract concern above it.

## Actuality versus what must be

The deepest mark of the difference is this. The scientist is in the business of discovering what *in fact* obtains. The philosopher is in the business of discovering what *must* obtain — what it would even make sense to claim could obtain. His interest in the actual is always strictly subordinate to his interest in the possible and the necessary. The chemist asks which reactions occur; the philosopher asks what it is for anything to *be* a reaction, a cause, an event — the constraints no possible reaction could violate.`,
