    body: `# How is analytic philosophy different from non-analytic philosophy?

John Stuart Mill (1806–1873), the great philosopher and economist, said that he was an expert in but one science: **the science of science.** What he meant was plainly similar to what we have been saying — and therefore similar to what Gottlob Frege (1848–1925), the great philosopher and mathematical logician, meant when he said that logic studies not the laws of nature but **"the laws of the laws of nature."** Whether Frege was strictly right depends on what exactly one means by "logic"; but if by "logic" he meant *philosophy*, his dictum was spot-on.

## Dummett's slogan

Frege is often described as the first analytic philosopher. Michael Dummett, an exceptionally capable philosopher of language, summed up the movement in a sentence: **"analytic philosophy is post-Fregean philosophy."** What does Dummett mean? To answer, we have to see what Frege changed.

## The pre-Fregean picture and its reversal

With some exceptions, pre-Fregean philosophers thought they were studying the most general features of the *actual* world. They took themselves to be in the same line of work as the botanist — saying how the world *is* — the only difference being that they treated more general features of reality than the botanist does. Philosophy, on this view, was a very abstract natural science.

Frege showed that this is wrong. Consider the botanist. Any interest he has in plants that *might* exist but don't is subordinate to his interest in the plants that *actually* exist. Like all scientists, he is interested in what there could be only to the extent that it helps him figure out what there is. **But with philosophers it is the other way around: any interest they have in the actual is subordinate to their interest in the possible.** That reversal of priorities — the possible over the actual rather than the actual over the possible — is precisely what makes philosophy a different *kind* of inquiry, not merely a more general natural science.

## Sense as the gateway to possibility

But how can one investigate the possible without leaving one's chair? Here is Frege's method. Unlike non-analytic philosophers, analytic philosophers figure out what there *could* be by **analyzing statements.** The crucial link is this: statements that *make sense* are exactly those that *can* be true, and statements that don't make sense are those that can't. Therefore statements that make sense describe *possible* realities, and those that don't, don't. To survey the space of statements that make sense is thus to survey the space of genuine possibilities — an inquiry the analytic philosopher can carry out entirely by reflecting on what can sensibly be said, without gathering any new empirical data at all.`,
